WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.515
Why humans stay in the loop.

2
00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:12.056
Weeks 1 to 3 built the machinery: error analysis, code graders, LLM judges calibrated against human labels, and statistics for comparing systems.

3
00:00:12.336 --> 00:00:15.856
Every one of those pieces rests on a human judgment somewhere.

4
00:00:16.136 --> 00:00:24.968
The judge is only as good as the labels you calibrated it against, and the test set is only as good as the failures someone noticed and wrote down.

5
00:00:25.248 --> 00:00:35.168
Eugene Yan’s essay on eval process makes the point bluntly: an LLM judge does not rescue a product whose team has stopped looking at outputs.

6
00:00:35.448 --> 00:00:40.525
Automated evaluators scale a review process that already exists; they don’t replace it.

7
00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:45.349
Anthropic’s guide to agent evals describes the same idea as layers.

8
00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:58.621
Automated evals catch known problems fast, production monitoring shows real behavior, user feedback surfaces surprises, and manual transcript review builds the intuition that tells you what to automate next.

9
00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:00.971
No single layer catches everything.

10
00:01:01.251 --> 00:01:04.152
There is also a subtler reason humans stay involved.

11
00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:07.653
Shankar and colleagues (“Who Validates the Validators?

12
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:14.525
”, 2024) observed criteria drift: people’s grading criteria changed as they graded.

13
00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:21.781
Reviewers needed criteria to judge outputs, but judging outputs is how they discovered what their criteria were.

14
00:01:22.061 --> 00:01:24.557
So the rubric you write on day one is a draft.

15
00:01:24.837 --> 00:01:32.069
Ongoing review is how it gets better, and how your judge’s calibration stays honest as the product and its users change.

16
00:01:32.349 --> 00:01:44.189
This lesson is about running that human layer well: who reviews, with what tool, on which traces, how user feedback fits in, how reviewed failures flow back into the test set, and what it all costs.
